<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01765</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>35d50e3c-816e-4335-8754-eab477955059</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="d58381a9-a116-4e2c-81b3-94b5b3cd37d1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Pattern A</para>
            <para>Here are two patterns of dots.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Four figures, labeled step 0 through 3. Step 0, no dots. Step 1, row of 4 dots. Step 2, 2 rows of 4 dots each. Step 3, 3 rows of 4 dots each.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Pattern B</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Four figures, step 0 through 3. Step 0, 2 dots. Step 1 row of 1 dot above a row of 2 dots. Step 2, 2 rows of 2 dots each, above a row of 2 dots. Step 3, 3 rows of 3 dots each, above row of 2 dots.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_10_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para> How many dots will there be in Step 4 of Pattern A?</para>
            <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! There will be 4 rows and 4 columns in Step 4.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.2.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 16.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bedc6d9a-4d0e-4890-901f-10b2428b8966" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many dots will there be in Step 4 of Pattern B?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The relationship is \(n^2+2\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.2.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 18.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_78ee8dd0-f16f-48fb-9102-636e1d540108" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which pattern shows a quadratic relationship between the step number and the number of dots?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The relationship is \(n^2+2\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.2.3 and 7.2.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Pattern B. This is because the relationship is \(n^2+2\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5df82e90-55ae-44d5-ad83-882333ba247f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Select two expressions that represent a quadratic relationship between the step number and the total number of dots. (If you get stuck, consider sketching the first few steps of each pattern as described by the expression.)</para>
            <para>Each expression represents the total number of dots in a pattern where \(n\) represents the step number.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.2.3 and 7.2.4.
     </para>
            <para>The correct answers are \(n^2\) and \(n \cdot n\).

     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2937ceb9-af8f-40b1-8969-71308b1f2a66" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Use the pattern below to answer the questions.</para>
            <para> How many blocks are in Step 4?</para>
            <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is always \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.
     </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.4. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 32.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6e40b6dc-235c-482f-a9cc-eeada42e60f2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many blocks are in Step 5?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is always \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.
     </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.4. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 44.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fdbe403b-0f9a-4799-ae16-bfce47321e47" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> How many blocks are in Step \(n\)?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(n^2-2\)</para>
            <para>\(n+6\)</para>
            <para>\((n+2)^2-n\)</para>
            <para>\((n+2)^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 – 7.3.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((n+2)^2-n\).

     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
